  "verbalization": "Mollicellin N is shown to interact with P2X purinoceptors, which are ionotropic receptors that mediate cellular signaling through purine ligand binding. This interaction is supported by a shared target of [(1R)-1-[(5Z)-4-Bromo-5-(Bromomethylidene)-2-Oxofuran-3-Yl]Butyl] Acetate, a compound that is a known inhibitor of P2X receptors. The compound [(1R)-1-[(5Z)-4-Bromo-5-(Bromomethylidene)-2-Oxofuran-3-Yl]Butyl] Acetate is also a known inhibitor of CHP-126, suggesting a potential mechanism of action involving P2X receptor modulation. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Mollicellin N may act through a common pathway, targeting P2X receptors and ultimately inhibiting CHP-126. The high scores in all three paths suggest a strong and consistent relationship between Mollicellin N and CHP-126, supported by a shared target and a known mechanism of action. The mechanism involves the modulation of P2X receptors, which are critical for cellular signaling and may underlie the therapeutic effects of CHP-126.",
